{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>What did this people do to you, etc.</b> The explanation is that since the prohibition against performing an act of idolatry is only a negative commandment (Rambam, Hilkhot Avodah Zarah, ch. 3), and one is liable only to lashes, and this is not among those things concerning which it is said 'be killed rather than transgress' — and anyone who was coerced is exempt. But there must be recognition that he was coerced, that they forced him; if not, he is liable to lashes even if he was not warned, for a colleague does not require a warning (Makkot 6:). Therefore he said to him, <b>what did this people do to you</b> — by way of coercion — <b>that you have brought upon it</b> etc., a great sin, in that you made for them idolatry." "And Aaron answered him: <b>you know the people, that it is set on evil</b> — meaning, their going astray (and their turning aside) attests that they are prone to ruin and corrupt. Further: do you suppose that I made the calf with my own hands? It was not so; rather, I threw the gold and it emerged on its own by means of a certain act, as they said, of blessed memory (Tanḥuma, Ki Tisa 19), that they performed sorcery. And from this it follows that even if there had been no coercion in the matter, he would not have transgressed the negative commandment, for he did not make with his hand any form or likeness." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may intend its saying <b>what did [this people do to you]</b> etc. — meaning, even if they coerced you with coercion sufficient to transgress the negative commandment, even so you should have weighed the great sin that would overtake them, and you should have borne the suffering rather than destroy a great people. And he answered him that he did nothing as described."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:08.435396188Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}